\section{Crossed Random Effects Analysis}
\label{app:crossed_re}

The one-way clustering in Appendix~\ref{app:robustness_methods} treats each judge position independently, but panels exhibit \emph{crossed} random effects: judge $j$ may occupy position~1 in one panel and position~2 in another, inducing a multi-membership structure. At $K{=}7$, each judge appears in $\binom{14}{6} = 3{,}003$ of $6{,}435$ panels (46.7\%), making crossed membership the dominant source of residual correlation.

We fit multi-membership cluster-robust variance estimators (CRVE) using a sandwich estimator with $t(G{-}1) = t(14)$ reference distribution, where $G{=}15$ is the number of judges. Table~\ref{tab:crossed_re} compares bootstrap and crossed RE significance.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{rcccc}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{Bootstrap}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{Crossed RE (FDR)}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \cmidrule(lr){4-5}
$\boldsymbol{K}$ & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ \\
\midrule
3 & 4/12 & 3/12 & 0/12 & 1/12 \\
5 & 7/12 & 2/12 & \textbf{8/12} & 3/12 \\
7 & 8/12 & 1/12 & \textbf{8/12} & 2/12 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Crossed random effects vs.\ wild cluster bootstrap significance (BH FDR $\alpha{=}0.05$). Multi-membership CRVE with $t(14)$ distribution. At $K{\geq}5$, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ retains 8/12 significance under crossed RE, matching or exceeding bootstrap. At $K{=}3$, the conservative $t(14)$ distribution with only 455 panels drives attenuation to 0/12 (raw $p$-values: 5/12 significant).}
\label{tab:crossed_re}
\end{table}

SE inflation increases with $K$ as judge overlap intensifies: $1.75$--$4.96{\times}$ at $K{=}3$, $6$--$12{\times}$ at $K{=}5$, $10$--$19{\times}$ at $K{=}7$. Despite this inflation, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ retains 8/12 significance at $K{\geq}5$ because the effect size is large enough to survive the correction. The $K{=}3$ attenuation (0/12) reflects two compounding factors: the conservative $t(14)$ reference distribution penalizes small-sample inference, and the effect sizes at $K{=}3$ are insufficient to overcome the inflated SEs. Raw (uncorrected) $p$-values show 5/12 conditions significant at $K{=}3$, confirming that the attenuation reflects degrees-of-freedom conservatism rather than absent effects.

\section{Extended Analysis}
\label{app:extended_analysis}

